2026-08-16T12:23:38Z    [ADI:Revenue] GATE critic: You misapplied management’s guidance: the stated midpoint for FY2026Q3 is $3.9 billion (3,900 USDm), but your blend anchors on 3,972.16 USDm—near the top of the ±$100m range—so the forecast is biased upward versus the actual guidance midpoint.
2026-08-16T12:24:02Z    [ADI:Adjusted diluted EPS] GATE guidance: 3.5115 is 6.4% away from the guided midpoint 3.3 with no explicit range to justify it
2026-08-16T12:24:02Z    [ADI:Adjusted diluted EPS] GATE critic: The blend is unit-inconsistent: you add a “seasonal_trend” of 11.6115 directly to quarterly EPS in USD/share. A $11.6115 per-share quarterly component is implausible versus ADI’s FY2026Q3 guidance of $3.30 ± $0.15 (range $3.15–$3.45) and likely represents either an annual EPS figure or an 11.6115% rate. This wrong-scale input inflates the result to 3.5115, even pushing it above the guided top end ($3.45). The seasonal term must be converted to a quarterly USD/share basis (or applied as a percentage to the base), not added as $11.6115.
2026-08-16T12:24:34Z    [ADI:Adjusted gross margin] GATE critic: You’ve double-counted FY2025Q3. For a FY2026Q3 forecast, the “last 4 reported” quarters are FY2026Q2, FY2026Q1, FY2025Q4, and FY2025Q3—so taking the mean of those four (70.85%) and then averaging it again with the prior-year same period FY2025Q3 (69.2%) gives FY2025Q3 five times the weight of any other quarter. That methodological error drags your base from 70.85% down to 70.025% before the +0.400 pp tilt, biasing the final 70.425% figure.
