2026-08-16T12:24:35Z    [HD:Net sales] GATE critic: You computed 47,502.1 USDm off a rounded anchor: FY2025Q2 is cited only as “$45.3 billion,” i.e., 45,300 USDm to the nearest $100m, not the exact net sales reported in the 10-Q. Using a base rounded to $0.1b (and a rounded 4.86% rate) to produce a result precise to the million overstates precision and almost certainly yields the wrong USDm figure. Pull the exact FY2025Q2 net sales (in USDm) and recompute; the answer will differ by tens to hundreds of millions.
2026-08-16T12:24:36Z    [HAS:Net fees] GATE critic: You’ve mixed bases. The -12.7% is a reported change derived from the actual GBPm figures (FY2024 1,113.6 to FY2025 972.4), but the -14.0% for FY2023→FY2024 is almost certainly Hays’ like‑for‑like/constant‑currency disclosure (how they usually present YoY net‑fee movements), not a reported change in GBPm. Averaging a reported change with an LFL change and then applying it to the reported FY2025 base (972.4 GBPm) puts the FY2026 forecast on the wrong basis. This is checkable against Hays’ FY2024 results, where the -14.0% is identified as LFL rather than reported.
2026-08-16T12:25:08Z    [HAS:Pre-exceptional basic EPS] GATE critic: The 37.772 “guidance anchor” you used is clearly the FY26 effective tax rate guidance (~38%) misapplied as an EPS level in GBp. ETR is a percentage, not an earnings-per-share figure, so blending 37.772 (a % tax rate) with any EPS inputs produces an EPS on the wrong basis. This is why the result (~36.62 GBp) simply mirrors the ~38% tax rate rather than any earnings metric.
2026-08-16T12:25:11Z    [HD:Adjusted diluted EPS] GATE critic: Wrong basis: the anchor “adjusted diluted EPS” of $4.68 for FY2025Q2 appears to be Home Depot’s GAAP diluted EPS (HD typically does not publish a non‑GAAP adjusted EPS in its releases/10‑Qs). If the $4.68 is GAAP, then your FY2026Q2 value is on the wrong basis for an “adjusted” metric.
2026-08-16T12:25:12Z    [DE:Worldwide net sales and revenues] GATE critic: You derived the -12.69% YoY by averaging prior YoY changes, but your own source says Q3 FY2025 worldwide net sales and revenues declined 9%, while you used -8.6% for FY2024Q3→FY2025Q3. That mismatch indicates you computed the growth rates on a different basis than “Worldwide net sales and revenues” (e.g., net sales or another adjusted measure) and then applied them to the GAAP "worldwide net sales and revenues" base. Using a mixed basis makes the $10,492.5m forecast wrong on basis.
2026-08-16T12:25:33Z    [HAS:Pre-exceptional operating profit] GATE critic: You’ve mixed bases: the only documented FY2026 figure is the FY-level consensus guidance of £45.2m (pre-exceptional operating profit), but you diluted it with a “seasonal_trend” input of 24.3277 that is not evidenced as an FY metric and is almost certainly a sub‑period figure. Blending an FY guidance number with a non‑FY seasonal value produces 44.5081 that is not on a consistent FY basis.
